  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Generally tranquil weather is expected to \nprevail through the early portion of next week. \n\nA dry cold front has moveed through the region. High pressure \nis now building in behind it and will remain through the middle\nof next week with continuing mild and dry conditions expected.\n\nConditions have become breezy in the wake of this morning's \ncold frontal passage. Northwest winds 10-15 mph with gusts near \n30 mph will continue. Temperatures will remain quite mild this \nafternoon though, as downsloping winds will allow for adiabatic \nwarming and drying out of the airmass. High temperatures will \nrange from the mid to upper 60s north of I-78 to the low to mid \n70s for most other areas south of there.\n\nConditions will turn cooler again for the weekend. Lows tonight\nthrough Sunday night will be mainly in the low 30s to mid 40s.\nWidespread frost currently appears unlikely where the growing \nseason continues, although we could see some frost development\nin the remaining PA and NJ counties. Highs will be coolest on \nHalloween, in the upper 50s to low 60s, under partly cloudy \nskies. Partly to mostly cloudy skies Sunday, but a bit milder \nwith highs in the low to mid 60s.\n\nScattered to broken cloud cover will persist into early next \nweek despite surface high pressure. Temperatures are expected to\nincrease back to above normal values early next week, with highs\nin the mid 60s to low 70s and milder overnights. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rain is expected to return late next week. \n\nThe stretch of dry weather is expected to come to an end late \nnext week as a period of rainfall is expected to move into the \narea as a storm system affects the region Thursday through at \nleast Friday. Early in the week, a cold front will be moving \nacross the central part of the country. The parent low moves \nacross the Great Lakes and into Canada later in the week, while \na secondary low develops and moves into the southern states by \nmid week. This low is expected to strengthen as it moves into \nthe Mid Atlantic and northeast states later in the week, \nbringing our area a period of rain.",
